WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Opaque tokens do not reveal their meaning to the client.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.160
The server maps the presented value to stored state.

3
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:22.620
That pattern is useful for API keys, reset tokens, invite links, magic links, email verification, and other workflows where the application wants direct control over status, expiration, owner, scope, and revocation.

4
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.880
Issuing the random value is only part of the design; managing the token record is the larger job.

5
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:41.960
A token record should say what the token is for, who owns it, what scope it carries, when it was created, when it expires, whether it is revoked, and which metadata supports monitoring or response.

6
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.460
One-time tokens should be invalidated after successful use.

7
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:54.080
API keys should be named, scoped, owned, monitored, rotated, and removable.

8
00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:01.220
Users usually should not be able to recover full API keys after initial creation unless there is a deliberate reviewed reason.

9
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:09.500
For many opaque token types, storing a cryptographic digest of the token reduces exposure if the database is read.

10
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.520
The application stores a verifier and checks the presented token against it.

11
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:19.940
This is especially useful for reset tokens, invite tokens, and some API key designs.

12
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:28.280
The digest is not a full solution by itself, but it reduces the chance that a copied database immediately becomes a set of live bearer secrets.

13
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.720
Token verification should avoid unnecessary information disclosure.

14
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:40.580
Lookup and error behavior should not reveal whether an account, email address, invite, or sensitive object exists.

15
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.920
Security-sensitive comparisons should use timing-aware patterns where appropriate.

16
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:49.580
The practical distinction is simple: a token is not just a random string.

17
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.000
It is a server-managed authority record with lifecycle state.
